# class Router { constructor(o) { this.update = o => { this.options = o; this.reset(o) }; this.update(o); fill(this, o) }   reset(o) { this.origin = o } }   function fill(router, o) { router.filled = o }
# What a constructor calls at every activation with the object goes on filling it: as receiver (`reset`), as argument (`fill`), or in a closure over the object that the constructor itself has made (`update`),
# and what those call in turn. What they store at every activation counts as filled by the constructor.
# A store in a branch of such a function (`inBranch`), a store by a function that the constructor calls in a branch (`rarely`), by a closure over the object that the constructor does not call (`later`)
# and by a function that gets the finished object (`other`) do not. The call of a closure takes what the closure holds out of it (GetCapture, which no text of this form has: a variable stands in for it)
# FORM: AsRead
# OPTIONS: only-passes=ResolveAccesses,EstimateHeat heat=EverythingIsHot
# RUN: plan-slots fill

